    "q-162933": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162933",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$\\frac{\\sqrt{125{t}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{5}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{125{t}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{5}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{125{t}^{4}}{5}}=\\sqrt{25{t}^{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{25{t}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(5{t}^{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ |5{t}^{2}| & = \\\\ |5|\\cdot |{t}^{2}| & = \\\\ 5\\cdot |{t}^{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot |{t}^{2}| & = \\\\ 5\\cdot {t}^{2} & = \\\\ 5{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
