    "q-162953": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162953",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{64{q}^{2}}}$ given that $q>0.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{64{q}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{\\sqrt{64{q}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{64{q}^{2}}}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{64{q}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{64{q}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(8q)^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{|8q|} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{|8|\\cdot |q|} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{8\\cdot |q|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $q>0,$ so we have $|q|=q.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8\\cdot |q|} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{8\\cdot q} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{8q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
